<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bahla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bahla is an oasis town in the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, at the foot of the Jebel Akhdar. It was the capital of the Nabhani dynasty from the 12th to the 15th century, and its great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud-brick</a> fort and some twelve kilometres of walls date largely from that period. The fort was inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1987, and the town is also known for its pottery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bahla.jpg" alt="Bahla" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francisco Anzola · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bahla_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bahla is a town in the Ad Dakhiliyah Governorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, about 40 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizwa">Nizwa</a> and about 200 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscat">Muscat</a>, the capital of Oman. It lies at the foot of Djebel Akhdar, where the fortresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rustaq">Rustaq</a> to the north and Izki, Nizwa and Bahla to the south have each been a capital at some time and so played an important part in Omani history. Most of its buildings are of traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud brick</a>, many of them hundreds of years old. The town and its fort are enclosed by the remains of a fortified wall 12 km long.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bahla's history is said to reach back to 3000 BCE, and its pottery to as early as 2500 BCE. The clay comes from the wadi, where men trample it to make it pliable; the finished object is then fired in a kiln. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> was a hub of the frankincense trade, and from the 16th to the 19th century its sultans ruled a trading empire stretching from the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> to the tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. In the mountains below Djebel Akhdar, Kharijite communities resisted all attempts at normalisation by the Caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harun-al-rashid">Harun al-Rashid</a> and put into practice religious concepts that were at once radically puritanical and democratic.</p><p>The oasis owed its prosperity to the Banu Nabhan, who from the mid-12th to the end of the 15th century imposed their rule on the other tribes. The adobe walls and towers of the great fort stand on a stone base. They probably include some elements from before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, but most of the construction dates from the time of the Banu Nabhan, and the latest reconstruction was at the beginning of the 16th century. At the foot of the fort, to the south-west, stands the Friday Mosque, whose sculpted mihrab probably dates from the 14th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bahla Fort is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and one of the largest forts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>. The old souq stands across the main road that leads to it, and its shops sell mostly pottery, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and other traditional handcrafted items. Bahla was long known for its Qur'anic schools and mosques and as a centre for scholars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imams</a>, among them Abu 'Ubaidah Abdullah bin al-Qasim in the 8th century and Sheikh Mohammed ibn Barakah in the 10th, whose school attracted students from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. Jabrin Castle, with painted ceilings and carved interiors, lies 5 km to the south-west.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bahla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ruling as maliks, or kings, from <b>Bahla</b> and its great fort, they governed the interior while the coast was dominated by Hormuz.</blockquote><a href="/state/nabhani-dynasty">Nabhani dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
